WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:04.637
-Whole first year of "SNL,"
I'm still living at home.

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:07.307
I remember the first time
I was on.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:10.442
I rode back to Long Island
on the Long Island Railroad,

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:13.279
and I didn't wipe my makeup off
because I was on TV.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.981
I thought people were gonna
see me with the makeup on

00:00:15.981 --> 00:00:17.217
and know I was on TV.

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:18.818
And, you know, no one knew.

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:22.321
I probably just looked like
a very strange, young fellow

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:24.223
on the train at night.

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:26.459
[ Laughs ]

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:30.463
♪♪

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.632
The late '70s, the early '80s,

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:34.767
there were three comedy clubs
in New York.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:38.137
There was Catch a Rising Star,
the Improv,

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:39.506
and the Comic Strip,

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.308
and I worked those clubs
before I came up here.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.543
That's how I found out
about this show was audition.

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:47.746
Everyone that was funny
came out, auditioned.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:50.850 line:0%
The first time I came here
was just Neil Levy,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.552 line:0%
just me and Neil in his office.

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:55.488 line:0%
And he said, "Make me laugh."

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.590
And, luckily, 'cause I was doing
stand-up already,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.926
so I had material,
and I did impressions.

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:02.528
I did Ali, Muhammad Ali...

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:04.264
-Getting in with me,
Cassius Clay!

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.132
The man's a disgrace to boxing.
He's too old to be fighting me.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.801
-And I did a Bill Cosby...

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.269
-You know you wanted to smile.
I saw you.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:13.406
You're like this.
I saw you.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.807
-And he didn't laugh at all.

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.943
He just sat
and kind of looked at me,

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.144
and he just said, "Thank you."

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.478
And then I left.

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:20.847
I was like,
"Well, I guess I didn't get it."

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:22.248
Then a couple weeks later,
they called me.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.250 line:0%
-And featuring Eddie Murphy!

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.886 line:0%
-It was hard at first,
for the simple fact

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.821 line:0%
that the show was so beloved.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:31.390 line:0%
When the whole cast just left --
Lorne left --

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.027 line:0%
and it was like, "Okay, we got
the hippest, hottest,

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.996 line:0%
funniest show on TV,
and we're gonna replace them

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.397 line:0%
with all these new faces."

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:40.800 line:0%
So, the audience was like,
"[bleep] all y'all

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.068 line:0%
and [bleep] this show
and [bleep]..."

00:01:43.068 --> 00:01:44.737
You would walk down the street,

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:46.205
and people were like, "You on
the new 'Saturday Night Live?'

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:47.474
Y'all ain't [bleep]"

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:50.109
So, we had a lot of that
the first year.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:51.777
It freaked a lot of the cast
out,

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.414
but I didn't get freaked out
'cause I'm, you know,

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:58.016
19 years old and just so happy
to be on TV.

00:01:58.016 --> 00:01:59.385
-This is live television!

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.988
-The first year of "SNL,"
I would still steal.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:05.258
I remember, I came back
from the Long Island Railroad,

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:06.860
and I had parked my car there,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.162
and somebody had stolen
my hubcaps.

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:10.863
I just stole somebody else's
hubcaps.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:14.366
And I was literally on the show,
and I was like this,

00:02:14.366 --> 00:02:16.602
taking somebody's...

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:18.003
[ Laughs ]

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:20.372
Like I said, the whole cast
was getting a lot of [bleep]

00:02:20.372 --> 00:02:21.574
They hated the show.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.010
And then they fired everybody.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.180 line:0%
Michael O'Donoghue,
he came and fired everybody.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.781
And he just kept me
and Piscopo.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.683
And the reason he kept me was
because there was a sketch

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:33.752
where there was an article
in the paper

00:02:33.752 --> 00:02:36.122
about old people eating
pet food,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.157
and I went to the news desk
and did some kind of thing

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:39.592
about it.

00:02:42.461 --> 00:02:45.498 line:0%
-Mmm-mmm!
Now, that's good holiday eating!

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.533 line:0%
-So, it looked like
I was eating dog food.

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:50.536
When O'Donoghue fired everybody,
and he came in, and he said,

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:52.238
"And the only reason
Murphy didn't get fired

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.539
is 'cause he ate that dog food!"

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.240
And I was like,
"That wasn't dog food.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.575
That was hash."

00:02:56.575 --> 00:02:58.310
And he looked at me like,
"What the [bleep]"

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.112
But it was too late,
and I kept the show.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:01.748
It was just that close.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:05.051
If he had known that was hash,
none of this would be happening.

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:06.252 line:0%
-Eddie Murphy! Eddie Murphy!

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.687 line:0%
Come on!
Let's hear it for him!

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:08.988 line:0%
Eddie Murphy!

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.057 line:0%
-You have this gun at your head
to be creative.

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.059
You have to get a character
that connects

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:14.527
or get something going.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:17.096
But I wasn't scared
'cause my stuff was working.

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:19.565
If you wrote something funny,
you can get it on.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:21.534
I mean, everything I wrote
didn't get on,

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.602
but the really funny stuff
got on.

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:25.838 line:0%
-[ Deep voice ] I been a junior
at Cleveland High

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:27.473 line:0%
going on seven years now.

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:29.007 line:0%
I'd like to tell you all

00:03:29.007 --> 00:03:30.677 line:0%
that this is the most disgusting
thing y'all done pulled

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:31.677 line:0%
up to date.

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:33.346 line:0%
Anytime we get something
going good,

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:34.681 line:0%
y'all got to move in on it.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.315
In the '60s,
we wore platform shoes.

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.451
Then y'all had to wear
platform shoes.

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.087
All I'm saying is that y'all
stay on the hockey courts

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:44.356
and the polo fields and let us
stay on the basketball courts,

00:03:44.356 --> 00:03:47.393
'cause if God would have wanted
whites to be equal to blacks,

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:52.331
everybody'd have one of these.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:55.000
-It still holds up.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.437
There isn't a better place
for me to have been

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.873
than, you know,
here at that age.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.842
That was the age you'd be
when you go to college.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:05.244
And this is, you know,
the equivalent of Harvard

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:06.812
for comic actors.

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.881
So, you're, like,
right in the middle of it.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:12.619
In my life, when I look back
on my experience here,

00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:16.054
before everything started,
as some of my fondest memories.

00:04:16.054 --> 00:04:18.257
I had a blast up here.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:20.192
I hope it's funny Saturday.

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:25.497
I hope it lives up to whatever
expectations anyone has.

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:28.367
I want to be
one of the funny ones.

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.503
[ Laughs ]

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.972 line:0%
Hey-hey-hey-hey!

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:34.740 line:0%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.842 line:0%
[ Crowd chanting,
"Eddie, Eddie, Eddie" ]

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:39.512 line:0%
Thank you!

